<p>The UK imported roughly USD 16.9 million of Indian fruit under HS 0804.50 in calendar 2024. That puts it in the premium-market tier alongside the US and Germany. Established UK Alphonso demand is driven largely by South Asian diaspora communities and, increasingly, by wider specialty-food retail. The HS heading also includes guava and mangosteen, so the figure is not mango-only. Mango is understood to make up the large majority of the value, but the classification does not allow a more precise claim. This guide is for UK importers, wholesalers and specialty retailers planning an April-July programme. It covers variety and grade specification, the documents each side handles, how price behaves, and the air-versus-sea decision.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Where does the UK sit between Gulf and US mango prices?</h2>
<p>In the middle, and closer to the premium end. The WITS/UN Comtrade dataset for calendar 2024 puts total Indian exports under HS 0804.50 at USD 154.4 million worldwide, about 107,295 MT, an average of about USD 1.44 per kg. Unit value varies sharply by destination: the United States imports at roughly USD 2.88 per kg and Saudi Arabia at about USD 0.90 per kg. This is not the same mango priced differently. It is two structurally different trades sharing one customs code. Western markets receive premium-variety fruit, mostly air-freighted and above all Alphonso and Kesar, sold into specialty and high-end retail. Gulf markets receive a higher-volume mix, often sea-freighted, at lower prices.</p>
<p>The UK took about 9,044 MT at about USD 1.86 per kg, close to Canada (USD 1.90) and above Germany (USD 1.67) and the UAE (USD 1.52), but well below the US. US-bound Indian mango also goes through irradiation, an extra treatment step and cost that the UK route does not carry. At the UK average, a 4kg carton was worth about USD 7.50 at export value, before freight and handling. Scenario: a London specialty grocer buying 8-10 count Devgad Alphonso by air should expect to pay well above that average, while a wholesaler buying 12-14 count Kesar for the Leicester and Birmingham trade will sit near it. Compare offers against your own variety and count, not against the country average.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>What variety and grade should a UK buyer specify?</h2>
<p>A mango quote is only comparable to another if both state the same variety, count grade and ripeness stage. Specify these items:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Variety and origin</strong>: Alphonso (Hapus) or Kesar. Each has a distinct flavour and market position, and Alphonso commands the premium in most UK specialty channels. Alphonso is grown along the Konkan coast, with Ratnagiri and Devgad the best-known origins; name the one you sell under.</li>
  <li><strong>Size/count grading</strong>: 8-10, 10-12 or 12-14 count per 3-4kg box, matched to your retail or food-service format. Larger, more uniform fruit sits at the top of the price range.</li>
  <li><strong>Eating quality</strong>: Brix 18%+ at ripeness for Alphonso. The fruit should be free from fruit-fly damage, black spot, bruising and split skin.</li>
  <li><strong>Ripeness at dispatch</strong>: set by the transit method, colour-turning for air and mature-green for sea. The comparison table at the end of this guide sets out the air-versus-sea trade-off.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong>: 3kg, 4kg or 5kg single-layer cushioned corrugated cartons, or telescopic export cartons for premium air-freight orders. Single-layer packing matters because stacked fruit bruises.</li>
  <li><strong>Pesticide residue testing</strong>: strongly recommended before shipment because UK maximum residue limits are strict.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When is the Alphonso season, and how early should UK buyers book?</h2>
<p>Alphonso and Kesar mango are available from April to July. There is no meaningful off-season supply of fresh fruit, so this is not a product where enough notice lets us source it at any time. Early- and late-season fruit behave differently from mid-season peak volume in both price and consistency. If you are planning a UK mango programme, build your buying calendar around this window and book delivery weeks early. Our <a href="/resources/harvest-calendar/">harvest calendar</a> shows the wider seasonal picture across our product range.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How many cartons fit in a reefer, and does the voyage outlast the fruit?</h2>
<p>A 40ft reefer takes about 16-18 MT of mango and a 20ft about 8-10 MT, because single-layer cartons fill the box before it reaches its weight limit. In 4kg cartons that is about 4,250 cartons in a 40ft at 17 MT and about 2,250 in a 20ft at 9 MT. At the 2024 UK average export value, the 40ft load is worth about USD 31,700 and the 20ft about USD 16,800.</p>
<p>The harder number is time. Fruit under controlled reefer conditions of 10-13°C keeps about 2-3 weeks, and the voyage to Southampton or Felixstowe takes 18-24 days. On those figures a sea shipment uses most or all of the fruit's controlled-temperature life before it docks, before customs clearance and distribution. That is why air, at about a day in transit, is the default for Alphonso, and why sea only makes sense for mature-green fruit, a buyer with its own ripening rooms and a sales plan for all 2,250 or 4,250 cartons within days of arrival. Scenario: a Birmingham wholesaler that sells about 500 cartons a week would need more than eight weeks to clear a 40ft of Alphonso; it is better served by weekly air consignments.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<p>For the UK, your importer or their agent files the IPAFFS pre-notification before the consignment arrives. The details on the phytosanitary certificate have to match the consignment and the pre-notification exactly, because a mismatch holds fruit at the border, and for a perishable product that costs shelf life. Where the destination rules require hot water treatment, the HWT record goes with the phytosanitary paperwork. A pre-shipment pesticide residue report is not always mandatory, but it is the practical safeguard against a residue finding after landing. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>
<p>Timing is where first orders slip. Under the GB rules, plants and plant products from non-EU countries fall into high, medium A, medium B or low risk categories set under the Border Target Operating Model (BTOM), and APHA carries out the plant health checks in England and Wales. The IPAFFS pre-notification has to be in at least 4 working hours before an air consignment lands, or at least 1 working day before a sea consignment arrives, and the phytosanitary certificate must be issued no more than 13 days before or after the consignment leaves India. Confirm mango's current risk category with your importer or APHA before the season. Scenario: an air shipment packed on a Friday lands at Heathrow early on Sunday. If the importer planned to file IPAFFS on Monday morning, the fruit waits in the cargo shed losing selling days; filing on Friday, as soon as the airway bill number is known, avoids it. Treatment rules also differ by market: the US route uses irradiation and some Asian markets require vapour heat treatment (VHT), so tell us the destination before packing and we confirm whether hot water treatment applies.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

